  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${8}^{2x}={10}^{x-7}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2x} & ={10}^{x-7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({8}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({10}^{x-7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)=(x-7)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) & =(x-7)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) & =x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)-7\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)-x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10) & =-7\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) & =7\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)) & =7\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10) \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
